{"name":"Monument Green","hex":"#d3c190","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"monument","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A structure built for commemorative or symbolic reasons, or as a memorial; a commemoration.","An important site owned by the community as a whole."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To mark or memorialize with a monument.","To place a surveyor's monument at."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/monument"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Monument","blocks":[{"text":"A monument is a type of structure created to commemorate a person or event, or which has become relevant to a social group as a part of their remembrance of historic times, or cultural heritage, due to its artistic, historical, political, technical or architectural importance. Examples of monuments include statues, (war) memorials, historical buildings, and cultural assets. If there is a public interest in its preservation, a monument can for example be listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Monumento_ao_Cristo_Redentor.jpg/330px-Monumento_ao_Cristo_Redentor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monument","wikidata":"Q4989906"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Monumento_a_la_Bandera_2.jpg/500px-Monumento_a_la_Bandera_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Monumento_a_la_Bandera_2.jpg/1280px-Monumento_a_la_Bandera_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Monumento_a_la_Bandera_2.jpg/500px-Monumento_a_la_Bandera_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"National Flag Memorial in Rosario, Argentina","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMonumento_a_la_Bandera_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Braniborsk%C3%A1_br%C3%A1na.jpg/500px-Braniborsk%C3%A1_br%C3%A1na.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Braniborsk%C3%A1_br%C3%A1na.jpg/1280px-Braniborsk%C3%A1_br%C3%A1na.jpg?u